    {
      "kind": "section",
      "text": "1 Introduction",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000012"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "text": "The performance and stability of modern machine learning models increasingly depend on the geometric structure of their matrix parameterizations; traditional constraints such as symmetry or orthogonality have been foundational, while recent work leverages manifold theory to map unconstrained spaces onto constrained geometries like the Birkhoff polytope, which enforces doubly stochastic constraints but faces a trade-off between exactness and scalability.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000013"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "Birkhoff polytope"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "manifold theory"
      ],
      "mentions": [
        "doubly stochastic matrices",
        "symmetry",
        "orthogonality"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Constraining attention patterns to doubly stochastic matrices improves accuracy in vision and language models and interprets learned parameters as probability trajectories.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000014"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "doubly stochastic matrices",
        "vision models",
        "language models"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Sinkhorn Attention provides memory-efficient implementations due to its ability to mix internal representations and smooth optimization landscape.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000014"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "Sinkhorn Attention",
        "memory-efficient"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Residual connections are crucial for stabilizing training of deep neural networks and are a building block of modern large language models.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000015"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "deep neural networks",
        "large language models"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Dynamic generalizations of residual streams increase topological complexity by increasing the number of streams d while preserving the identity mapping property.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000015"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "residual streams",
        "identity mapping"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "figure",
      "text": "Figure 1: Spectral analysis and architectural integration of go-mHC.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000016"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "text": "Figure 1 illustrates spectral comparison showing go-mHC approximates the Birkhoff polytope within epsilon(s) error using d(d‑1)/2 parameters; mHC‑lite provides full coverage but requires d! parameters; KromHC is restricted to B₂ and disallows cycles >2. The parameterization maps skew‑symmetric X to orthogonal Q via the Cayley Transform, then projects to doubly stochastic B via block‑wise Frobenius norm. Hyper‑Connections ℋ_res is integrated into the residual stream as x_{l+1}=ℋ_l^{res} x_l + Layer F(x_l), where the doubly stochastic constraint ensures training stability and improved gradient flow.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000017"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "Hyper‑Connections",
        "ℋ_res"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "Birkhoff polytope",
        "Cayley Transform"
      ],
      "mentions": [
        "epsilon(s)",
        "d(d‑1)/2 parameters",
        "d! parameters",
        "B₂",
        "Frobenius norm",
        "gradient flow"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Manifold‑Constrained Hyper‑Connections (mHC) shows that constraining residual connections to the interior of the Birkhoff polytope significantly improves training stability and scalability.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000018"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "Manifold‑Constrained Hyper‑Connections"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "Birkhoff polytope"
      ],
      "mentions": [
        "training stability",
        "scalability"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Dynamic generalizations reveal a new scaling dimension: the number of residual streams d.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000018"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "scaling dimension",
        "residual streams"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Follow‑up works have adopted mHC as the default backbone due to its superior modeling capabilities.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000018"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "default backbone",
        "modeling capabilities"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "Exact parameterizations have been introduced to prevent error accumulation across layers (Yang and Gao 2026; Zhou et al. 2026).",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000019"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "error accumulation",
        "exact parameterizations"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "mHC‑lite uses the Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem to parameterize ℋ_res as a convex combination of all d! permutation matrices.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000019"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "mHC‑lite"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem"
      ],
      "mentions": [
        "convex combination",
        "permutation matrices"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "The parameter count O(d·d!) explodes super‑exponentially; for d=8, 8! = 40 320 permutations are required, making this approach memory‑prohibitive without sampling approximations.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000019"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "parameter count",
        "memory‑prohibitive",
        "sampling approximations"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "KromHC employs Kronecker products of small doubly stochastic matrices to achieve exact constraints with O(d²) complexity, but at the cost of severely reduced expressivity.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000019"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "KromHC"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "Kronecker products"
      ],
      "mentions": [
        "exact constraints",
        "O(d²) complexity",
        "expressivity trade‑off"
      ],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source"
    },
    {
      "kind": "claim",
      "text": "The proposed solution is based on the algebraic theory of generalized orthostochastic matrices (Gutkin 2013; Nechita et al. 2023; Korzekwa et al. 2018; Shahbeigi et al. 2021).",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000020"
      ],
      "defines": [
        "generalized orthostochastic matrices"
      ],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [
        "algebraic theory",
        "references"
      ],
      "fills": [],
